{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"duration_ms":323740,"duration_api_ms":457600,"num_turns":22,"result":"","stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"eb3226fe-e933-4792-912c-8e9fe93b2a13","total_cost_usd":1.4667184500000003,"usage":{"input_tokens":16,"cache_creation_input_tokens":69913,"cache_read_input_tokens":733444,"output_tokens":9840,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":69913,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-opus-4-6":{"inputTokens":16,"outputTokens":9840,"cacheReadInputTokens":733444,"cacheCreationInputTokens":69913,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":1.04975825,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":64000},"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":1200,"outputTokens":16003,"cacheReadInputTokens":1823877,"cacheCreationInputTokens":122686,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.4169602,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000}},"permission_denials":[],"structured_output":{"summary":"Reviewed the full diff across substrate.rs, biology.rs, events.rs, shrimp.rs, scenarios/lib.rs, and all test files. The substrate oxic/suboxic zone implementation is sound: the Bouldin model is correctly applied, microfauna O2 demand is properly integrated, unit consistency holds throughout, and test coverage is thorough. The decomposer/nitrifier scaling split is well-motivated and correctly implemented. The per-stage molt cycle rewrite is mostly correct but has a concurrent-resolution logic issue. Found 3 substantive issues (1 correctness bug, 1 physics limitation, 1 maintainability risk) and no safety problems.","amendments":[{"body":"In `molt_cycle` (shrimp.rs ~lines 496-506), when multiple stages resolve on the same tick with different outcomes (some fail, some succeed), `last_molt_success` is unconditionally set to `false` by the failure branch but the `failed_molt_accum` accumulator net-decreases by 0.05 (increases 0.3 from failure, then decreases 0.35 from success). This creates an inconsistency: `last_molt_success` is read downstream as a sub-adult maturation gate (line 849), so a juvenile molt failure on the same tick as an adult molt success will block sub-adult maturation despite the accumulator showing improvement. Fix: either resolve per-stage outcomes independently (track `last_molt_success` per stage, use the sub-adult's own result for its maturation gate), or ensure the accumulator and boolean agree (e.g., only apply the success decrement to the accumulator when `!any_failed`).","rationale":"The population-wide `last_molt_success` boolean collapses per-stage outcomes into a single signal. When stages with different molt intervals happen to resolve on the same tick, failure always wins for the maturation gate but success always wins for the accumulator. This means sub-adult maturation can be spuriously blocked by a juvenile molt failure even though the sub-adults didn't molt, while the stress accumulator simultaneously signals improvement. The old single-timer code couldn't hit this case."},{"body":"In `effective_substrate_porosity` (substrate.rs lines 133-140), using only the first layer's porosity for D_eff in the Bouldin model produces incorrect penetration depths when the computed penetration extends past the surface layer into a deeper layer with significantly different porosity. For the common aquarium setup of a thin sand cap (e.g., 1 cm, φ=0.25) over deep aquasoil (φ=0.55), the model will underestimate penetration because it treats the entire bed as having the cap's low porosity even for depths beyond the cap. Consider a layer-by-layer iterative approach: apply the Bouldin equation within the surface layer, and if the result reaches the surface layer's full depth, continue into the next layer with its own D_eff for the remaining O2 concentration.","rationale":"The test `surface_layer_porosity_limits_heterogeneous_stack_penetration` validates that a low-porosity cap reduces penetration relative to a high-porosity cap, which is correct directionally. But the current implementation doesn't model the transition between layers at all — penetration in a [1cm sand, 5cm aquasoil] stack is computed as if all 6 cm are sand, even though 5/6ths of the bed is high-porosity. This matters because sand-capped aquasoil is one of the most common planted tank substrates and is likely the primary use case for the substrate zone model."},{"body":"In `estimate_substrate_microfauna_o2_demand_mg_per_s` (substrate.rs line 188), the detritus daily consumption fraction `0.01` is a magic constant not backed by any `process_params` field: `substrate_detritus_consumed_g = fine_detritus_g_total * 0.01 * population_index * substrate_fraction`. Unlike the periphyton consumption fraction which reads from `process_params.microfauna_periphyton_consumption`, this value is hardcoded and cannot be tuned via configuration. Extract it to a named constant with a documentation comment explaining the value's provenance, or add it to `ProcessParams` alongside the periphyton consumption parameter.","rationale":"The periphyton consumption fraction is correctly parameterized via `process_params.microfauna_periphyton_consumption`, but the parallel detritus consumption uses a bare `0.01` with no documentation or configurability. This asymmetry makes it easy for a future maintainer to miss that this value exists and needs tuning when calibrating microfauna behavior. It also makes the O2 demand calculation harder to audit since one consumption pathway is parameterized and the other is hidden inline."}]},"fast_mode_state":"off","uuid":"d3afa2f1-a36d-4ec6-9087-66d3e9cb452a"}
